## Accepted decisions — implement exactly

### D-03-1 — application-served static surface

Add an application-owned static asset surface under `/static/` with this
contract:

- methods: `GET` and `HEAD` only;
- caller: public; no authentication, capability, session or CSRF requirement;
- trusted-host validation still applies;
- the surface remains available while the portal kill switch is active, so
  maintenance, login and safe error pages can retain their presentation;
- assets are same-origin and remain compatible with N-26; no CDN, remote font,
  remote script or remote image origin is permitted;
- path traversal, directory listing and serving files outside the one approved
  static root must be impossible;
- missing assets return a safe `404` without exception text or filesystem paths;
- fingerprinted assets receive immutable public caching; non-fingerprinted
  assets receive a conservative short-lived or no-cache policy documented in
  the operational contract;
- static responses must not set or refresh session, login-transaction or CSRF
  cookies; and
- the closed structural inventory must explicitly account for Starlette mounts
  as well as ordinary method routes. A mount may not remain invisible to
  TC-STRUCT-01.

Record the exact URL grammar, static filesystem root, cache policy, host and
kill-switch behavior in the route and operational contracts. Keep static-file
plumbing in the backend package; do not add production CSS, HTMX, images or
visual assets. If framework construction requires the static directory to
exist, add only the smallest non-visual repository-owned placeholder necessary
and explain it.

### D-03-2 — define `ConfirmScope`

Define `ConfirmScope` in the frozen `vm-1` contract using the accepted
implementation shape, without renaming, removing or inventing fields:

- `preview_token`
- `checksum_full`
- `folder`
- `profile_version`
- `expires_at`
- `would_create`
- `would_update`
- `blocked`

Document exact types, bounds, safety/provenance rules and when the value is
present. Preserve the rule that R-46 submits `preview_token` and `nonce`; the
checksum, folder, profile and aggregate versions are re-read server-side and
must not become browser authority.

### D-03-3 — define `CharacterFilters`

Define `CharacterFilters` in `vm-1` exactly as implemented:

- `query: SafeText | None`
- `include_inactive: bool`

Document its bounds and that display-name search is a search fact, never an
identity or authorization fact.

### D-03-4 — record VM-13 CSRF accurately

Add VM-13's implemented `csrf_token` to the view-model contract. Preserve the
synchronizer-token design required by N-17. Do not introduce cookie-readable
CSRF, double-submit CSRF or client-side token authority.

Correct or remove the implementation docstring claim that this addition was
recorded in the P3.2 submission; no supporting occurrence exists there. Do not
rewrite the accepted historical P3.2 submission to manufacture provenance.

### D-03-5 — correct R-36

Make the route contract internally consistent:

- R-36 returns `200` HTML;
- it renders VM-13 with `state="denied"` and
  `additional_provider="no_additional_provider"`;
- it is not a `303` redirect; and
- Phase 3 still has exactly one ordinary identity provider.

This is a contract-record correction matching accepted runtime behavior. Do not
add another provider or linking flow.

### D-03-6 — dedicated safe denial view

Introduce a dedicated typed `DeniedView` containing only:

- `state`; and
- a closed-vocabulary `reason`.

Replace the use of VM-02/`NonMemberView` as the generic denial carrier at every
portal and import route boundary. Do not add correlation IDs, guild names,
timestamps, object identifiers, exception text or free-form reasons to
`DeniedView`.

Preserve these invariants exactly:

- an object the caller cannot reach and an absent object produce byte-identical
  `404` response bodies and equivalent relevant headers;
- denial categories remain closed vocabulary;
- authorization happens before object lookup where the accepted contract
  requires it;
- the distinct non-member page may continue using VM-02 with its intentionally
  permitted membership-recovery context; and
- the dedicated denial view does not weaken existing `401`, `403` or `404`
  status behavior.

Assign and document the new view-model identifier consistently. Treat it as an
additive `vm-1` extension unless the accepted versioning rules demonstrably
require otherwise; if they do, stop and return the conflict to Peter instead of
silently changing the version.

